2	 * no-flag-params — a boolean flag argument splits one function into two
3	 * behaviours selected at the call site. `render(true)` tells the reader
4	 * nothing; `renderCompact()` tells them everything. The house style wants one
5	 * function per behaviour, named for what it does.
6	 *
7	 * What counts, on the declaration side: a parameter whose TS annotation is
8	 * `boolean` (also `boolean | undefined` / `boolean | null`), or whose default
9	 * value is a `true`/`false` literal. On the call side: a bare `true`/`false`
10	 * literal passed as an argument to a call or `new` expression.
11	 *
12	 * Option `allowNamed` (default true): object-destructured parameters
13	 * (`{ force }: { force: boolean }`) and object-literal arguments
14	 * (`save({ force: true })`) are named, so they read at the call site and are
15	 * not flags. Set it to false to report those too.
16	 *
17	 * Detect-only: the fix is a second function, or a discriminated options
18	 * object — human judgment, not a codemod.
19	 */
